# Khadas Edge1 (RK3399) - vendor file contexts.

###############################
# Device nodes
#
# Mainline names. /dev/mali0 belonged to the BSP's midgard kmod; panfrost is a DRM
# driver, so the GPU is the render node. /dev/mpp_service, /dev/vpu_service,
# /dev/rga and /dev/iep were Rockchip's own char devices; rkvdec and RGA are V4L2
# devices here, which AOSP already has a type for.
# Block devices, by the names first-stage init gives them.
#
# /dev/block/by-name/<name> exists only for the controller named in
# androidboot.boot_devices (init/devices.cpp:410-421), which U-Boot or the bootfs
# script sets to the medium actually booted - so these label the booted medium's
# partitions and nothing else. ueventd labels the device node itself through the
# best match over its symlinks, so the node gets the type too.
#
# Nothing had a type here before, which left every partition as the generic
# block_device. Permissive SELinux on a userdebug bring-up hides that; an enforcing
# build would fail at first-stage mount of metadata and at every vold and fs_mgr
# access to userdata. The types are AOSP's public ones, the same set GloDroid's
# RK3399 port labels. vbmeta has no public type and stays block_device.
/dev/block/by-name/super            u:object_r:super_block_device:s0
/dev/block/by-name/metadata         u:object_r:metadata_block_device:s0
/dev/block/by-name/userdata         u:object_r:userdata_block_device:s0
/dev/block/by-name/misc             u:object_r:misc_block_device:s0
/dev/block/by-name/boot             u:object_r:boot_block_device:s0
/dev/block/by-name/recovery         u:object_r:recovery_block_device:s0

# gpu_device for the KMS node too, not graphics_device: minigbm's mapper opens card0
# in every app process (rockchip-drm has no render node), and apps may use
# gpu_device but not graphics_device. card1 is panfrost's primary node. GloDroid
# labels both the same way.
/dev/dri/card0                      u:object_r:gpu_device:s0
/dev/dri/card1                      u:object_r:gpu_device:s0
/dev/dri/renderD128                 u:object_r:gpu_device:s0
# No /dev/video[0-9]* line. AOSP's own file_contexts has that exact spec, and the
# two files are concatenated before being compiled, so a second identical one is
# fatal:
#
#   file_contexts.concat.tmp: Multiple same specifications for /dev/video[0-9]*.
#   Error: could not load context file from ...
#
# Note "same specifications", not "same path": two different regexes that both
# match a path are normal and the most specific wins, which is why the /dev/dri
# lines above are fine next to AOSP's /dev/dri/card[0-9]* and
# /dev/dri/renderD[0-9]*. It is the identical regex text that is rejected.
#
# The media controller that comes with a stateless V4L2 decoder. ueventd.edge1.rc
# already sets its ownership; without a label here it keeps /dev's default type
# and no vendor domain can open it.
/dev/media[0-9]*                    u:object_r:video_device:s0
/dev/cec0                           u:object_r:hdmi_cec_device:s0
/dev/lirc0                          u:object_r:lirc_device:s0

###############################
# HAL services
#
# There are none to label. The Rockchip HIDL services this section used to point
# at - the rk3399 allocator, composer3, audio, tv.input, hdmi.cec, light, power,
# memtrack and Codec2 binaries - are gone with the BSP path, and the AOSP services
# that replace them carry their own labels in system/sepolicy. A vendor
# file_contexts entry for a binary that does not exist is not harmless: it names a
# type, and a type nothing else declares fails the policy build.

###############################
# Vendor binaries
#
# brcm_patchram_plus was labelled here. It is a vendor tool this tree no longer
# carries; see init.edge1.rc for where Bluetooth stands.

###############################
# Firmware / modules
#
# brcmfmac's firmware and the board NVRAM live here, and the kernel firmware
# loader reads them on behalf of the driver.
/vendor/firmware(/.*)?              u:object_r:vendor_firmware_file:s0
/vendor/bin/edge1-bootwatch\.sh      u:object_r:edge1_bootwatch_exec:s0

# minigbm and Mesa. The allocator service needs an exec type to get a domain: the
# ninth card's init refused to start it - "File /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.
# graphics.allocator-service.minigbm (labeled u:object_r:vendor_file:s0) has incorrect
# label or no domain transition", which init enforces even in permissive - and with
# no allocator nothing can draw. The libraries below are loaded into every process
# that draws (the mapper by gralloc clients, Mesa by every GL user), so they are
# same_process_hal_file, as AOSP already labels /vendor/lib*/egl. Names as minigbm
# and Mesa install them; GloDroid labels its minigbm variant the same way.
/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.graphics\.allocator-service\.minigbm  u:object_r:hal_graphics_allocator_default_exec:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/hw/mapper\.minigbm\.so      u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/hw/gralloc\.minigbm\.so     u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libminigbm.*\.so            u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libdrm\.so                  u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libglapi\.so                u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libgallium.*\.so            u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/dri(/.*)?                   u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
# No /vendor/lib/modules: this kernel builds every driver it needs in, so there is
# no vendor_dlkm and nothing to load.
